# Veripsa release gate (auto-discovered). One gate per file -> two PRs adding gates add two
# FILES, never a shared line -> no false merge conflict on registration. register_gate is
# defined in run_gates.sh. Format: register_gate <test> <marker> <name> <ok_blurb> <desc> [tail]
register_gate "tests/test_reserve_lanes_atomic.py" "RESERVE-LANES ATOMIC GATE: PASS" "reserve_branch_lanes atomic rollback" "reserve_branch_lanes atomic rollback (small-findings root fix — verified LIVE, not by inspection): reserve_branch_lanes loops over a push's changed paths calling act_for_claim_with_authority once per path with NO per-path try/except, RELYING on make_db_processor's atomic-per-event transaction (autocommit=False around handle_event; commit on success, ROLLBACK + re-raise on any error). This gate INJECTS a REAL gate error mid-loop (act_for_claim is replaced by a wrapper that RAISES on a poison path sitting BETWEEN two real paths, delegating to the renamed original otherwise) and drives a multi-path push through the REAL make_db_processor: it proves the mid-loop raise (B) RE-RAISES out of the processor (-> failed -> GitHub redelivers) and (C) the WHOLE event ROLLS BACK ATOMICALLY — ZERO lanes for the branch, the EARLIER path's reservation discarded too (no partial lane reservation persists) — and (D) a clean retry after clearing the fault reserves all lanes (no half-state wedged it). CONCLUSION: the existing atomic-per-event design is correct and load-bearing; a per-path guard is NOT needed (and would be harmful — it would swallow the raise and let a partial reservation commit)" "reserve_branch_lanes atomic rollback (a mid-loop gate error rolls back every earlier path's lane reservation as a unit and redelivers — proven by real fault injection through make_db_processor; no partial commit) — write-integrity" 18
